// Companion to test_bare_return_main: `return <value>` from an untyped main
// must keep compiling (main is emitted as `long long wyn_main()` and the value
// becomes the process exit status).
//
// This test's comment used to read "the runner only checks stdout, so behavior
// is encoded via prints before/after the return" — the prints below are that
// workaround. The claim was true, and that was a harness defect, not a fact to
// design tests around: the accept path discarded the program's exit status
// entirely, so a test that printed its expected output and then panicked was
// scored PASS. With the status now checked (default 0), this test is the one
// file in the corpus that legitimately exits non-zero, and it asserts the
// thing it previously could not: that `return 2` from main really does become
// exit status 2. (2026-07, status assertion added 2026-10)
fn main() {
    println("before")
    if 1 == 1 {
        println("returning")
        return 2
    }
    println("unreached")
}
// EXPECT: before
// EXPECT: returning
// EXPECT_EXIT: 2
